      "dc:title": "STABILITY INVESTIGATIONS OF ROCK SLOPES IN CANADIAN MINING PROJECTS",
      "dc:creator": "BRAWNER CO",
      "prism:publicationName": "West Miner",
      "prism:volume": "43",
      "prism:issueIdentifier": "9",
      "prism:pageRange": "31-41",
      "prism:coverDate": "1970-01-01",
      "prism:coverDisplayDate": "Sept",
      "dc:description": "Open Pit mines in Canada presently extended to depths of 800 ft in bedrock and several mines are being proposed with excavation depths of 1500 to 2500 ft. To minimize mining costs there is a great incentive to excavate the pit slopes as steep as possible. The methods presently used in Canada to evaluate rock slope stability are outlined. Investigations to evaluate typical stability problems are summarized for four mining projects, that encompass a surface mine in which an estimated 400,000 cu yd rock slide occurred, a redesign of pit slope in aurface coal mine, influence of seepage on slope stability, and a preliminary design of pit slope for a proposed 2500 ft deep surface copper mine.",
